Neutralisation, $\mathrm{H^+(aq)} + \mathrm{OH^-(aq)} \rightarrow \mathrm{H_2O(l)}$, forms a new covalent O-H bond and releases energy: $\Delta H \approx -57\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ for a strong acid and a strong base.

The others are not exothermic:

- B: the ionisation of a weak acid is almost thermoneutral (about zero), and certainly not clearly exothermic;
- C: adding a second electron to $\mathrm{O^-}$ is strongly endothermic (about $+800\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$), since the electron is repelled by the negative ion;
- D: the first ionisation energy of magnesium is endothermic ($\approx +738\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$): energy is needed to remove an electron. **Answer: A.**

Reading the diagram:

- the products are **40** units below the reactants, so the forward reaction is exothermic, $\Delta H_{\text{forward}} = -40\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$;
- the peak is **20** above the reactants, so the activation energy of the forward reaction is $E_a(\text{forward}) = +20\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$;
- the reverse reaction goes from the products up to the same peak: $\Delta H_{\text{reverse}} = +40\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ and $E_a(\text{reverse}) = 20 + 40 = 60\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$.

Options A ($+40$ for the forward reaction: wrong sign), B ($+20$: this value is the activation energy, not an enthalpy change) and C ($+20$ for the reverse reaction: it is $+40$) are all false. The statement of option D is cut off after "The activation" in the printed paper, so it cannot be checked. If it said that the activation energy of the reverse reaction is $60\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ it would be the only true one, but this is not printed.



